{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1535223-monteparnas-crossover-rules","id":1535223,"title":"Monteparnas' Crossover Rules","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-05-07T11:33:30-07:00","id":1535223,"title":"Monteparnas' Crossover Rules","content":"The rules I'll put on here have four main distinct goals:<br /> <br /> 1- Explore what happens when a city is under command of a given splat; but others still pop around.<br /> <br /> 2- Mix social structures of the splats. Sometimes the city is so cosmopolitan that distinctions almost disappear. Sometimes just a single character is &quot;fostered&quot; by other denizens of the night.<br /> <br /> 3- Include two or more splats in the PCs' group, be it because the game is a crossover, but even more for the opposite: to explore all of them from the perspective of a single gameline.<br /> <br /> 4- Being easy to plug and play. Those are not Vectors, no new social splats or a Beast with family ties. Instead, those rules are meant for use on any chronicle to add characters, playable or not, that crossover, without forcing the whole chronicle to be something otherwise unintended.<br /> <br /> With those goals in mind, I'll present two sets of rules. The first is the crossing over social splats: what happens when a werewolf joins a Covenant in lieu or in addition to tribe or lodge?<br /> <br /> And the second is the crossing over Integrity trait, a means to bring different splats under the themes of your chronicle without loosing coherence.<br /> <br /> Other posts will come with the actual rules as I make them. Sometimes I'll also explore the uses of those rules and the types of games they should help create. I'm also open to opinions and suggestions, specially because I won't be fast working, and help is always welcome to mitigate this.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-10T16:36:59-07:00","id":1535422,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:16px\">When Covenants accept the Uratha</span><br /> <br /> <br /> Sometimes a werewolf joins a Covenant and the All Night Society. Maybe the Uratha are too few in their city, or the Kindred just found the nuzusul first. Maybe they are powerful enough to demand even the Uratha to pledge allegiance to their political houses, or the Uratha are so powerful that they demand the Covenants to accept an observer with status within. Maybe it's just a local thing or political move. <br /> Most of the time this is a simple matter. The werewolf is inducted as an effective Status 0 member and participates on open events and parties, but aside that is dedicated to their own society. Those who want (or have no choice) can even fully dedicate themselves to the Covenant, gaining Status and access to their benefits as if the Covenant was a Lodge (there's no distinction in practice).<br /> <br /> On rarer occasions, though, specially if the Uratha has three or more on Covenant Status and no link or contact with the usual tribes, it happens that their relationship with the Covenant runs as deep as that of a Tribe. And the Spirit responds in kind.<br /> <br /> No one knows how it happens. Some suggest that the Firstborn help those who have no way to know and contact the tribes. Some say there are strange Firstborn that pledged themselves to the Covenants when those were created. Some say an old and powerful enough Vampire that lives in the Hisil for a time can become just like the Firstborn. Some search for an answer, within the Covenants or the Hisil, and most just accept.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Carthian Movement</b></span><br /> <u>As a Lodge</u>, Carthians teach their experiments and results, giving access to Carthian Law. If a given Law affects only Vampires, it is adjusted to be able to also affect Uratha, like taking Essence if it would take Vitae. At Storyteller discretion, some Laws are a Breaking Point towards Flesh when taken, or even when used, as they may be more meaningful in Uratha society.<br /> <br /> <u>As a Tribe:</u><br /> \u200c<u><b>Prey: </b></u>Carthians promote the application of human solutions to inhuman problems, and favor the development of new experiments. For them, the most deserving prey are the monsters who establish dominance through monstrous power, squashing social change through strength alone.<br /> \u200c<u><b>Renown: </b></u>Glory<br /> \u200c<u><b>Gifts: </b></u><i>Rage</i>, Inspiration, Evasion.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Circle of the Crone</b></span><br /> <u>As a Lodge</u>, they teach the secrets of <b>Cr\u00faac</b>. It doesn't cap <b>Harmony</b> as it does <b>Humanity</b>, but every rite learned is a <u>Breaking Point towards Spirit</u>. Also, the very first Vitae point in the cost can be exchanged by Essence, but the others must be provided by a vampire as usual. Otherwise it works as normal <b>Cr\u00faac</b>.<br /> <br /> <u>As a Tribe:</u><br /> \u200c<u><b>Prey: </b></u>the Circle is, above all else, an alliance of minority religions, old and new, against the depredations of bigger and culturally meaner players. Their prey are cultural predators that socially and symbolically target gods and tries to destroy their cults.<br /> \u200c<u><b>Renown: </b></u>Wisdom<br /> \u200c<u><b>Gifts: </b></u><i>Nature</i>, Elemental, Insight.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Invictus</b></span><br /> <u>As a Lodge</u> the First Estate gives access to Oaths, as even the most stubborn Notary prefers to not deny them to a Covenant member, at least as Vassal. Invictus Uratha are also likely lieges for Oaths sworn by other Uratha. On some Domains the requisite for an Uratha to be a Notary may be raised to Status 4, but must won't go that far in denying recognition to a standing member.<br /> <br /> <u>As a Tribe:</u><br /> \u200c<u><b>Prey: </b></u>the Invictus ostensibly exists to keep the Masquerade. Any prey that becomes a threat to the Secret also becomes the most deserving of Siskur-Dah.<br /> \u200c<u><b>Renown: </b></u>Honor<br /> \u200c<b><u>Gifts: </u></b><i>Death</i>, Dominance, Stealth.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Lancea et Sanctum</b></span><br /> <u>As a Lodge</u>, they teach the secrets of Theban Sorcery. It doesn't need minimum Harmony as it does Humanity, but every rite learned is a Breaking Point towards Flesh. Otherwise it works as normal Theban Sorcery.<br /> <br /> <u>As a Tribe:</u><br /> \u200c<u><b>Prey: </b></u>the Spear preaches the role of its members as holy predators that test the kine and eliminates those found wanting. The most deserving prey is the monster, even if human in all but action, that by itself preys on the innocent.<br /> \u200c<u><b>Renown: </b></u>Purity<br /> \u200c<u><b>Gifts: </b></u><i>Weather</i>, Knowledge, Warding.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Ordo Dracul</b></span><br /> <u>As a Lodge</u>, the Order seems at first to have little to offer, but that's misleading. First, they do give access to laboratories where the member can research new Coils and Scales appropriate to their species, as it may still illuminate new paths for their research to follow.<br /> <br /> Second and more concrete, they have access to Dragon Nests, and more than a few of those are Loci. Being with the Order means access to a network of Loci out of reach to other Uratha and the power of the Covenant to guard them.<br /> <br /> <u>As a Tribe:</u><br /> \u200c<u><b>Prey: </b></u>the dragons want knowledge above all else, knowledge about any and everything. As long as your goal is to access either new knowledge or new and interesting Nests or test subjects, it is a worthy hunt.<br /> \u200c<u><b>Renown: </b></u>Cunning<br /> \u200c<u><b>Gifts: </b></u><i>Technology</i>, Shaping, Strength.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-11T10:05:45-07:00","id":1535446,"title":"","content":"Can someone throw me ideas on how to make Tribes &amp; Lodges benefit Vampire members? I'm not sure if access to Gifts would be an adequate effect, although it may be.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-11T16:29:16-07:00","id":1535469,"title":"","content":"Well, it's hard t shake of the V:tM, bias, against lupines, but, how can the Uratha help? Won't VII be surprised. <br /> <br /> Think of what a vampire wants; immortality. A vampire in a constant struggle, won't get to live out their eternity of nights. Tribe or lodge or gift, that can maintain stability and tranquility, will be most welcome.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-11T18:38:55-07:00","id":1535474,"title":"","content":"I'm running aground of a particular problem here, which is that the social apparatus of Uratha society exists for reasons directly antithetical to the reasons vampires form Covenants. It makes very little sense for a set of institutions built to keep werewolves the right distance from the world of humanity to offer any benefit to holding a vampire's Humanity above the dropoff point, which, coupled with the generally goal-oriented nature of werewolves, means Kindred are mostly going to get benefits relating to their supernatural qualities.<br /> <br /> A cost break on Devotions relating to different Disciplines per tribe would seem to involve the least amount of finicky homebrew, but more particular recommendations would require an understanding of what you think vampires in the Tribes and in Lodges are like.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-11T21:38:25-07:00","id":1535480,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1535223-monteparnas-crossover-rules?p=1535474#post1535474\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm running aground of a particular problem here, which is that the social apparatus of Uratha society exists for reasons directly antithetical to the reasons vampires form Covenants. It makes very little sense for a set of institutions built to keep werewolves the right distance from the world of humanity to offer any benefit to holding a vampire's Humanity above the dropoff point, which, coupled with the generally goal-oriented nature of werewolves, means Kindred are mostly going to get benefits relating to their supernatural qualities.<br /> <br /> A cost break on Devotions relating to different Disciplines per tribe would seem to involve the least amount of finicky homebrew, but more particular recommendations would require an understanding of what you think vampires in the Tribes and in Lodges are like.</div> </div> </div> </div>As a rule of thumb the idea is that for whatever reason the Vampire will join a Tribe, they're going to be a proper tribe member, with the same goals and responsibilities, so the difference in the reasons those societies exist are less a problem and more the whole point. This vampire is living as a part of Uratha society.<br /> <br /> My idea is to have mechanics similar to the original splat, so it is as little of a break as possible, but embracing the new themes wholeheartedly.<br /> <br /> Letting them buy Tribal Gifts would be the simplest and, in a sense, perfect answer, as that is exactly what Covenants do: access to buy new mechanics. But I'm not sure if this will work well. It's also in the air if it would let them buy Renown (but no free dot, and Gifts would be only the Tribal ones).<br /> <br /> Devotions are an option, but would be harder work.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-12T01:31:57-07:00","id":1535484,"title":"","content":"The problem is that the most logical part of Uratha society, and the easiest mechanical one to slot vampires into, is the pack, not the Tribe. Wolf-Blooded aren't part of Tribes even if some of them associate with a Tribe for various reasons. Wolf-blooded are parts of Packs though. Even humans can join a Pack. Extended that to vampires is a simple thing to do, and comes with direct benefits vampires could enjoy that don't run afoul of crossover issues because wolf-blooded and human Pack members need to be able to benefit as well.<br /> <br /> Focusing on the Pack level of things doesn't seem to be your ideal, but it hits your stated list of design goals in post 1 far more than trying to turn the Covenants into Lodges or Tribes will, because those are groups that only benefit Uratha members directly, and thus are not simple plug-and-play ways to easily handle Werewolf and other splat integration into one social structure.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-12T13:13:08-07:00","id":1535492,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:16px\">A Vampire within Uratha Society</span><br /> <br /> Werewolves work in extended groups, part gang, part family, part cult, whole recognized by the strange mystic nature of the Shadow. And they can welcome anyone to be a part. They also have Tribes and Lodges that can't extend so easily the magic within, but can nonetheless be powerful and influential allies to a non-Uratha &quot;member&quot;.<br /> <br /> Vampires may end up joining a Pack for almost any reason someone would join a group or organization, from politics to safety to them being their actual living family. The price may be a lot steeper if they want the strongest benefits, as they may be required to participate in the Hunt. It does let them access a lot of advantages, though, and some may want to be there with their buddies anyway.<br /> <br /> Tribes and Lodges don't accept such creatures as easily, the prospect Vampire having to prove their dedication to the Hunt of their favored prey in accordance to Siskur-Dah. Most candidates are members of Packs, although exceptions are possible. They're usually a sign that either the group is very politically active in the region and interact with the Covenants in such capacity, or the Kindred is already obsessed with the Hunt. Who are they to judge?<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Benefits of a Pack</b></span><ul><li>\u200cAs long as the Uratha consider the Kindred a member, the Undead receives the benefits of the <b>Pack Totem</b>, no strings attached, and can participate in <b>Pack Rites</b> as a Human would.</li> </ul><ul><li>If the Vampire is helpful enough in the Hunt and the Pack and Totem are welcoming, they can contribute to the <b>Totem Merit</b>. If they do so, they're considered <b>Wolf-Blooded</b> and can buy their <b>Merits</b>.</li> </ul>Obs.: the rules aren't clear on when Wolf-Blooded really need the <b>Pack Bond Merit</b> to purchase the Totem Merit. If the Storyteller requires it, then the Vampire can buy Pack Bond as a Wolf-Blooded.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Joining a Tribe or Lodge </b></span><ul><li>\u200cA Vampire successfully accepted as a member can buy <b>Status</b> on the organization. Usually this stops at 1, but it can be increased at Storyteller discretion. With Status 1 the Vampire has every benefit and obligation a normal member would.</li> </ul><ul><li>\u200cIf the Vampire has both <b>Status</b> in the Tribe or Lodge and <b>Allies</b>, they can petition for some supernatural help in the form of the favored or exclusive Gifts and Rites of the group. If it would be an available favor, its Rating is at -1. If not, it becomes available with a rating of the level of the specific power + 2, or lower. It may take some time for the Uratha to arrive.</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":5826,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","user":"monteparnas","date":". 05-15-2024, 08:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-12T13:20:41-07:00","id":1535494,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:16px\">A Vampire within Uratha Society</span><br /> <br /> Werewolves work in extended groups, part gang, part family, part cult, whole recognized by the strange mystic nature of the Shadow. And they can welcome anyone to be a part. They also have Tribes and Lodges that can't extend so easily the magic within, but can nonetheless be powerful and influential allies to a non-Uratha &quot;member&quot;.<br /> <br /> Vampires may end up joining a Pack for almost any reason someone would join a group or organization, from politics to safety to them being their actual living family. The price may be a lot steeper if they want the strongest benefits, as they may be required to participate in the Hunt. It does let them access a lot of advantages, though, and some may want to be there with their buddies anyway.<br /> <br /> Tribes and Lodges don't accept such creatures as easily, the prospect Vampire having to prove their dedication to the Hunt of their favored prey in accordance to Siskur-Dah. Most candidates are members of Packs, although exceptions are possible. They're usually a sign that either the group is very politically active in the region and interact with the Covenants in such capacity, or the Kindred is already obsessed with the Hunt. Who are they to judge?<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Benefits of a Pack</b></span><ul><li>\u200cAs long as the Uratha consider the Kindred a member, the Undead receives the benefits of the <b>Pack Totem</b>, no strings attached, and can participate in <b>Pack Rites</b> as a Human would.</li> </ul><ul><li>If the Vampire is helpful enough in the Hunt and the Pack and Totem are welcoming, they can contribute to the <b>Totem Merit</b>. If they do so, they're considered <b>Wolf-Blooded</b> and can buy their Merits.</li> </ul>Obs.: the rules aren't clear on when Wolf-Blooded really need the <b>Pack Bond Merit</b> to purchase the Totem Merit. If the Storyteller requires it, then the Vampire can buy Pack Bond as a Wolf-Blooded.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Joining a Tribe or Lodge</b></span><ul><li>\u200cA Vampire successfully accepted as a member can buy <b>Status</b> on the organization. Usually this stops at 1, but it can be increased at Storyteller discretion. With Status 1 the Vampire has every benefit and obligation a normal member would.</li> </ul><ul><li>\u200cIf the Vampire has both <b>Status</b> in the Tribe or Lodge and <b>Allies</b>, they can petition for some supernatural help in the form of the favored or exclusive Gifts and Rites of the group. If it would be an available favor, its Rating is at -1. If not, it becomes available with a rating of the level of the specific power + 2, or lower. It may take some time for the Uratha to arrive.</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":5826,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","user":"monteparnas","date":". 05-15-2024, 08:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}